@@s001 Code: interval scheduling in Java
@@s002 Code: interval scheduling in Java
@@s003 Earliest finish time in Java: an array of intervals, a sort with a lambda, and the greedy scan.
@@s004 Reading and sorting
@@s005 Each interval is an array of two integers, <code>{start, end}</code>. I sort the array of arrays by the end.
@@s006 Reading and sorting
@@s007 A two-dimensional array: <code>n</code> rows, each with two columns. <code>iv[i][0]</code> is the start, <code>iv[i][1]</code> the end.
@@s008 I sort by column 1, that is the end. The <b>lambda</b> <code>(x, y) -> …</code> is the comparison function.
@@s009 Syntax notes
@@s010 <code>Integer.compare(a, b)</code> returns −1, 0 or 1. Do not use <code>a − b</code>: with large numbers it overflows and the comparison becomes wrong.
@@s011 A lambda is an unnamed function passed as an argument: here it implements the <code>Comparator</code> interface.
@@s012 The greedy scan
@@s013 I go through the intervals in order of end. Once I take one, I update the end of the last one taken.
@@s014 The greedy scan
@@s015 End of the last chosen interval. At the start it is the minimum integer: no interval blocks the first one.
@@s016 <i>for-each</i> over an array of arrays: <code>c</code> is a row, that is an interval.
@@s017 Compatible: the new interval does not start before the end of the last one taken.
@@s018 I update the end: it is the new barrier for the following ones.
@@s019 Syntax notes
@@s020 <code>String.join(" ", scelti)</code> joins the elements of a list with a separator.
@@s021 <code>"[" + c[0] + ","</code>: the concatenation with <code>+</code> converts the <code>int</code>s to text.
@@s022 Running it
@@s023 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
